\section{The subcommands}

Every chapter that provides a command contributes it here.

<<imports>>=
import fbf.discussions
import fbf.progress
import fbf.har
import fbf.reviews
<<functions>>=
def add_subcommands(subp):
    """Add all fbf subcommands to the argparse subparsers `subp`."""
    fbf.discussions.add_command(subp)
    fbf.progress.add_command(subp)
    fbf.har.add_command(subp)
    fbf.reviews.add_command(subp)
    for parser in subp.choices.values():
        parser.set_defaults(func=reporting_errors(parser.get_default("func")))
@

The [[for]] loop wraps every command so that our errors end the program with
their message, not a traceback.
The messages were written for the user: a [[LaunchError]] names the hop, a
[[SchemaError]] the missing name and the recovery procedure.
A traceback would bury them, and [[canvaslms]] only reports its own errors
this way.
The wrapper keeps the original function in [[__wrapped__]], which the
standalone program uses below.

<<imports>>=
import functools
import canvaslms.cli
from fbf.api import APIError
from fbf.launch import LaunchError
from fbf.schema import SchemaError
<<constants>>=
REPORTED_ERRORS = (LaunchError, APIError, SchemaError, LookupError)
<<functions>>=
def reporting_errors(command):
    """Wrap `command` to exit with the message of our errors."""
    @functools.wraps(command)
    def wrapper(config, canvas, args):
        try:
            return command(config, canvas, args)
        except REPORTED_ERRORS as error:
            canvaslms.cli.err(1, str(error))
    return wrapper
@


\section{Inside [[canvaslms]]}

For [[canvaslms]] we add a single [[fbf]] command with the subcommands below
it, so that they do not mix with [[canvaslms]]'s own.
[[canvaslms]] then needs only
\begin{center}
[[fbf.cli.add_command(subp)]]
\end{center}
among its other commands, preferably guarded by a [[try]] around the import,
so that [[fbf]] stays optional.

<<functions>>=
def add_command(subp):
    """Add the `fbf` command, with its subcommands, to `subp`."""
    parser = subp.add_parser(
        "fbf", help="Detailed data from FeedbackFruits",
        description="Detailed data from FeedbackFruits activities.")
    add_subcommands(parser.add_subparsers(
        title="fbf commands", dest="fbf_command", required=True))
@


\section{The standalone program}

The standalone program does what [[canvaslms]]'s [[main]] does for its
commands, reusing its parts.
It reads the same configuration file, so that [[canvaslms login]] is all the
setup [[fbf]] needs.
It takes the same global options for the configuration file and the
delimiter.

<<imports>>=
import argparse
import canvaslms
import canvaslms.cli.cache
<<functions>>=
def main():
    """Run the standalone `fbf` program."""
    argp = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="fbf",
        description="Detailed data from FeedbackFruits, through Canvas.")
    argp.add_argument(
        "-f", "--config-file",
        default=f"{canvaslms.dirs.user_config_dir}/config.json",
        help="canvaslms configuration file, run `canvaslms login` to set up")
    argp.add_argument("-d", "--delimiter", default="\t",
                      help="field delimiter, default tab")
    add_subcommands(argp.add_subparsers(
        title="commands", dest="command", required=True))
    args = argp.parse_args()
    config = canvaslms.cli.read_configuration(args.config_file)
    <<run [[args.func]], with Canvas if it needs it>>
@

The HAR commands read files and need no Canvas; creating a Canvas object
for them would demand credentials for nothing.
We recognise them by the wrapped function.
[[canvaslms]] faces the same question for its own commands, and it cannot
see through our wrapper, so we answer it in a function that both programs
call: [[canvaslms]] adds [[not fbf.cli.needs_canvas(func)]] to its list of
commands that need no Canvas.

<<constants>>=
COMMANDS_WITHOUT_CANVAS = {fbf.har.har_shape_command,
                           fbf.har.har_diff_command}
<<functions>>=
def needs_canvas(func):
    """True unless the fbf command `func` works without a Canvas object."""
    return getattr(func, "__wrapped__", func) not in COMMANDS_WITHOUT_CANVAS
@

The others get the Canvas object from [[canvaslms]]'s cache, which also
caches course and assignment lists between runs, so we save it afterwards as
[[canvaslms]] does.
<<run [[args.func]], with Canvas if it needs it>>=
if not needs_canvas(args.func):
    args.func(config, None, args)
    return
try:
    canvas, hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.cache.load_configured_canvas(
        config)
except canvaslms.cli.cache.CanvasCredentialsError as error:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1, str(error))
try:
    args.func(config, canvas, args)
except (canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError,
        canvaslms.cli.MultipleMatchesError) as error:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1, str(error))
finally:
    canvaslms.cli.cache.save_canvas_cache(canvas, token, hostname)
@

Let's verify the predicate the way both programs use it: on the function
that the parsers set for a command line.
We parse through [[add_command]], as [[canvaslms]] would, so that the test
also covers the nesting under [[fbf]] and the wrapping of every command.
<<test [[cli.py]]>>=
import argparse

import fbf.cli

def command_of(*argv):
  parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  fbf.cli.add_command(parser.add_subparsers(dest="command"))
  return parser.parse_args(["fbf", *argv]).func

def test_only_the_har_commands_work_without_canvas():
  assert not fbf.cli.needs_canvas(command_of("har-shape", "x.har"))
  assert not fbf.cli.needs_canvas(command_of("har-diff", "x.har"))
  assert fbf.cli.needs_canvas(command_of("discussions"))
  assert fbf.cli.needs_canvas(command_of("progress"))
